From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc3: qcom: select the phy mux before the reset

On IPQ5018 the USB pads are shared with PCIe through a mux in TCSR.
The selection was made near the end of probe, after the controller had
already been through its reset and after the clocks were up.

The vendor driver (QSDK dwc3-qcom.c, qcom,multiplexed-phy) writes the
selection as the very first thing in probe, ahead of the usb30 master
reset and the clock setup. Match that: the SuperSpeed pads are muxed
away for the whole reset otherwise, and the link never leaves
Rx.Detect. USB2 does not go through this mux, which is why high speed
worked throughout.

Measured on a TP-Link Archer AX55 v1: with this change a USB3 stick
enumerates at SuperSpeed and hdparm reports 112-116 MB/s, against
~100 MB/s under the vendor firmware.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom.c
@@ -779,6 +779,18 @@
 	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, qcom);
 	qcom->dev = &pdev->dev;
 
+	/*
+	 * Select the USB position of the phy mux before anything comes out
+	 * of reset. The vendor driver does this as the first thing in probe,
+	 * ahead of the controller reset and the clocks; selecting it later
+	 * leaves the SuperSpeed pads muxed away while the controller resets,
+	 * and the SS link then never leaves Rx.Detect (USB2 is unaffected,
+	 * it does not go through this mux).
+	 */
+	ret = dwc3_qcom_setup_phy_mux(qcom);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
 	qcom->resets = devm_reset_control_array_get_optional_exclusive(dev);
 	if (IS_ERR(qcom->resets)) {
 		return dev_err_probe(&pdev->dev, PTR_ERR(qcom->resets),
@@ -826,10 +838,6 @@
 	if (ignore_pipe_clk)
 		dwc3_qcom_select_utmi_clk(qcom);
 
-	ret = dwc3_qcom_setup_phy_mux(qcom);
-	if (ret)
-		goto clk_disable;
-
 	ret = dwc3_qcom_of_register_core(pdev);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(dev, "failed to register DWC3 Core, err=%d\n", ret);
